{
  "sample": "internet-java-grpc-examples",
  "$comment": "Hand-labeled from the pinned sample source (scripts/samples.json), not from Concentrate output. The sample is the grpc-java repository; scope is examples/src/main/java (the five Java files of the helloworld and routeguide examples) and examples/src/main/proto (the four protos). The generated gRPC and protobuf classes (GreeterGrpc, RouteGuideGrpc, Point, Feature, ...) are not in the scan. Rpcs: all 10 rpcs of the four protos with their messages as written and streaming kind. The methods that implement an rpc are the overrides in a class that extends the generated `<Service>ImplBase`: HelloWorldServer.GreeterImpl.sayHello and the four RouteGuideServer.RouteGuideService methods; Echo and StreamingGreeter have no server in this sample. served is null (no generated Go interface). Calls: every call site of the five files whose callee is a function, method or explicit constructor of this repository (including this(...) constructor calls), labeled with its definition (checked by searching each in-repository method name in the five files); calls into gRPC, protobuf, Guava and the JDK, and `new X()` of a class without an explicit constructor (GreeterImpl, HelloWorldServer, TestHelper), are not labeled, so any target they get counts as a false positive. A call through a generated stub (blockingStub.sayHello, asyncStub.recordRoute) runs the service implementation, which is its target (as in the C++ sample); a call on the TestHelper interface has the interface method as its only target (no implementation in the scan). Imports: the non-JDK imports of the five files, all null (generated classes, gRPC, protobuf, Guava: nothing resolves inside the scan); JDK imports are not labeled, so a resolution of any import counts as a false positive. Env: the Java sources read no environment variable or system property (no getenv or getProperty call in the sample's Java code), so the scope has no items and any USES_ENV from a Java file is a false positive.",
